! The displaced silicon of si2-nc-force.in, relaxed back onto its site.
!
! The relaxation this project's tests are held to: the answer is known in
! advance -- diamond silicon's second atom sits at (1/4, 1/4, 1/4) and nothing
! else -- so the final geometry can be checked against the crystal as well as
! against QE. The starting point is 0.02 alat away along x, which is far enough
! that BFGS takes several steps and near enough that it cannot end up somewhere
! else.
!
! The atoms both move: the force on them is equal and opposite, so what returns
! to (1/4, 1/4, 1/4) is their *separation*, while the pair drifts along x by
! half the displacement. That is not a defect of the relaxation -- a periodic
! crystal has no preferred origin, and the total force being zero is what says
! so.
 &control
    calculation = 'relax'
    tprnfor = .true.
 /
 &system
    ibrav=2, celldm(1)=10.20, nat=2, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-8
 /
 &ions
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.27 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 4 4 4 0 0 0
